WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.900
Proposition forty-three changes the vote threshold for certain local

2
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:07.300
taxes.

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.020
A special tax pays for a specified purpose, such

4
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:11.940
as a local service.

5
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.980
A general tax supports general government spending.

6
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.780
This measure focuses on special taxes proposed by voters.

7
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.100
Government-proposed local special taxes generally need two-thirds approval.

8
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.340
Court decisions allow voter-proposed special taxes to pass by

9
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.640
a simple majority.

10
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:37.360
Proposition forty-three would require two-thirds for those voter proposals

11
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:37.820
too.

12
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.600
Imagine residents discussing a proposal for local services.

13
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.440
Suppose a hundred people vote on a citizen-proposed special

14
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.600
tax, and sixty vote yes.

15
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.320
That is enough for a simple majority.

16
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.240
It is short of two-thirds, so the same proposal

17
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.440
would fail under the new requirement.

18
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.700
Local services and the tax burden are both at

19
00:00:58.700 --> 00:00:59.080
stake.

20
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.700
Beginning January first, twenty twenty-seven, the higher threshold would

21
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.140
apply to new, increased, or extended local special taxes

22
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.420
proposed by voters.

23
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.180
An extension matters because some existing taxes expire unless

24
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.440
voters renew them.

25
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.080
The proposition does not set a new tax rate

26
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.640
or immediately cancel every existing tax.

27
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.900
It changes the hurdle future proposals must clear.

28
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.800
Voters would still decide whether a particular tax deserves

29
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.480
support.

30
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.140
The analyst expects local revenue could be lower than

31
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.860
it otherwise would be, but gives no fixed amount.

32
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:41.300
The effect depends on which future measures receive majority

33
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.500
support but fall short of two-thirds.

34
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.700
A voter might want broad agreement before a dedicated

35
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.600
local tax takes effect, regardless of whether a council

36
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.540
or a citizen group proposes it.

37
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.960
They may see one consistent threshold as protection for

38
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.100
taxpayers who would have to pay despite voting no.

39
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.560
You might ask how much power a minority should

40
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:03.880
have.

41
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.160
Another voter could object that more than half the

42
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.460
community may support a service but still be unable

43
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:11.160
to fund it.

44
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.020
In the example, forty no votes block sixty yes

45
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:15.320
votes.

46
00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.140
A higher hurdle can protect dissenters while making collective

47
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.020
action harder.

48
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.760
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 43

49
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.680
percent said yes, 51 percent no, and 6 percent

50
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.080
were unsure.

51
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.390
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

52
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.160
A later September Berkeley survey found 31 percent yes,

53
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.420
40 percent no, and 29 percent undecided.

54
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.540
Question wording and methods differ, so comparing polls does

55
00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.480
not by itself establish a trend.

56
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.440
Yes raises the threshold for these voter-proposed special taxes;

57
00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.200
no retains the current majority route.

58
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.140
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

59
00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.520
and official voter guide.

60
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.140
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
